          "content": "Elias had lived on the Needle for twenty years, a jagged spire of rock jutting out of the grey Atlantic. His life was measured in the rhythmic rotation of the Fresnel lens and the endless, salt-crusted silence of the coast. He liked the silence; it didn't ask for things he couldn't give.\n\nThe map arrived not by mail or by hand, but by the tide.\n\nAfter a particularly violent autumn gale that had shaken the lighthouse to its foundations, Elias went down to the tide pools to scavenge for driftwood. There, wedged inside a barnacle-encrusted copper tube, was a",
